# Opensya Core

> Runtime & framework layer built on top of NestJS using a convention-driven architecture.

## Vision

`@opensya/core` is not a classic NestJS application.

It is a **runtime engine** that transforms a simple file-based structure into a fully working NestJS application.

The goal is to provide:

- a modular backend architecture
- a convention-over-configuration workflow
- automatic registration of controllers/services/models
- a simplified developer experience
- strong TypeScript integration
- scalable project organization
- dynamic runtime capabilities

Instead of manually wiring modules, providers and controllers everywhere, Opensya Core analyzes your project structure and builds the application automatically.

---

# Philosophy

Traditional NestJS applications require a lot of manual configuration:

- registering controllers
- importing modules
- wiring providers
- configuring routes
- exposing metadata
- managing dependency trees

Opensya Core moves this logic into a centralized runtime/compiler layer.

The developer focuses on:

- business logic
- folder organization
- conventions

The framework handles:

- discovery
- compilation
- registration
- runtime orchestration

---

# Global Architecture

```text
server/
├── controllers/
├── services/
├── models/
├── guards/
├── locales/
```

At startup:

1. Opensya loads the project configuration
2. The runtime scans the filesystem
3. Definitions are compiled
4. NestJS metadata is generated dynamically
5. The Nest application is created automatically

---

# Runtime Lifecycle

```text
nestEntry()
    ↓
loadConfig()
    ↓
load environment
    ↓
nestCompiler()
    ↓
scan server/*
    ↓
register definitions
    ↓
generate runtime metadata
    ↓
createApp()
    ↓
NestJS application starts
```

---

# Core Concepts

## 1. Convention over Configuration

The framework relies heavily on conventions.

Example:

```text
server/controllers/user/get.ts
```

Automatically becomes:

```http
GET /user
```

Another example:

```text
server/controllers/auth/login/post.ts
```

Becomes:

```http
POST /auth/login
```

No explicit controller registration is required.

---

# 2. Dynamic Compilation Layer

The project contains an internal compiler/runtime system.

This layer is responsible for:

- scanning files
- resolving metadata
- creating runtime definitions
- building NestJS-compatible structures

This is why Opensya Core behaves more like a framework runtime than a simple NestJS wrapper.

---

# 3. File-Based Backend Routing

Routes are inferred from the filesystem.

Example structure:

```text
server/controllers/
├── user/
│   ├── get.ts
│   ├── post.ts
│   └── [id]/
│       └── get.ts
```

Generated routes:

```http
GET    /user
POST   /user
GET    /user/:id
```

HTTP methods are inferred from filenames.

---

# 4. Dynamic Service Registration

Services are automatically registered into the runtime container.

Example:

```ts
export default defineService(function (id: string) {
  const model = getModel('User');
  const user = await model.findById(id);

  return user;
});
```

The runtime:

- discovers the service
- registers it
- exposes typings
- injects it into the application lifecycle

---

# 5. Runtime Globals

The runtime exposes several global variables internally.

Examples:

```ts
_config;
_env;
_nestConfig;
_nestApp;
logger;
```

These globals centralize runtime state and orchestration.

This architecture simplifies dynamic compilation and internal framework coordination.

---

# 6. Type Generation

The framework generates additional typings dynamically.

This allows:

- service autocompletion
- runtime-aware typing
- dynamic module inference
- framework-level DX improvements

Example:

```ts
const service = useService('user.get');
```

With full inferred typing.

---

# 7. NestJS as the Execution Engine

NestJS is used as the underlying execution platform.

Opensya Core adds:

- discovery
- compilation
- orchestration
- metadata generation
- runtime abstraction

NestJS remains the transport/runtime engine underneath.

---

# Developer Experience

The framework aims to provide:

- less boilerplate
- faster backend development
- scalable structure
- modular organization
- strong typing
- filesystem-driven architecture

---

# Example

## Controller

```ts
export default defineController({
  async handle({ req }) {
    return {
      message: 'Hello world',
    };
  },
});
```

File:

```text
server/controllers/hello/get.ts
```

Generated route:

```http
GET /hello
```

---

# Internal Layers

## Runtime Layer

Responsible for:

- globals
- lifecycle
- orchestration
- bootstrapping

---

## Compiler Layer

Responsible for:

- file discovery
- metadata extraction
- dynamic registration
- route generation

---

## Nest Layer

Responsible for:

- actual HTTP execution
- DI container
- middleware/interceptors/guards
- transport layer

---

# Why This Architecture?

This architecture provides:

- rapid backend prototyping
- cleaner project structures
- easier scaling
- highly modular applications
- framework extensibility
- plugin/runtime capabilities

It also opens the door for:

- distributed modules
- runtime plugins
- hot module loading
- dynamic package registration
- framework tooling
- code generation

---

# Important Note

Opensya Core is closer to:

- Nuxt backend runtime
- Next.js API routing
- Nitro runtime
- AdonisJS conventions

than to a classic manual NestJS architecture.

NestJS is the foundation.

Opensya Core is the orchestration layer.

---

# Current Status

The project is currently focused on:

- stabilizing the runtime
- improving typing generation
- strengthening the compiler layer
- building the testing infrastructure
- improving developer tooling

